cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference this QA study, please cite it as below. The original proofs belong to OpenAI; cite their repository for the mathematics."
type: dataset
title: "Scientific QA Evidence: an independent QA audit of OpenAI's Navier–Stokes and Euler Lean proofs, with Lean bridges to Clay's problem statement and checker controls"
authors:
  - family-names: Guerra
    given-names: Cristian
    alias: criguex
    city: Medellín
    country: CO
repository-code: "https://github.com/criguex/scientific-qa-evidence"
url: "https://github.com/criguex/scientific-qa-evidence"
version: "1.3.0"
date-released: 2026-10-22
abstract: >-
  A software-QA audit package. It rebuilds OpenAI's Lean 4 formalizations of
  finite-time blowup for Navier–Stokes (Clay alternatives C and D) and Euler from
  a pinned commit, rechecks them with Comparator using the Lean kernel and nanoda
  on five builds, tests the statement check with mutants, and proves in Lean that
  OpenAI's theorems imply Clay's (C) and (D) as written literally in Lean, a
  rendering not yet independently reviewed. Pre-registered controls on three
  Mathlib theorems separate a changed statement from a false one, and a paired
  macOS/Linux mutation-testing campaign covers nine synthetic numerical models
  (84 variants per host: 27 PASS, 54 KILLED, 3 SURVIVED). It claims no new
  theorem and no scientific certification.
keywords:
  - software testing
  - quality assurance
  - reproducibility
  - mutation testing
  - formal verification
  - Lean 4
  - Navier–Stokes
